{
  "summary": "This review examines strategies to enhance spatial resolution in liquid cell transmission electron microscopy (LCTEM) by addressing electron scattering from membranes and liquid layers. It evaluates MEMS-based designs, ultrathin windows, and liquid layer thickness optimization to achieve higher resolution imaging while maintaining dynamic process observation in liquid environments.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Reducing liquid and membrane thicknesses minimizes electron scattering",
    "Optimizing MEMS architectures improves imaging quality",
    "Grid-based liquid cells enable high-resolution dynamic observations"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work advances LCTEM by systematically addressing resolution limitations through material and design innovations, enabling atomic-scale imaging of liquid-phase processes with enhanced structural and dynamic resolution."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated an injectable ROS-responsive hydrogel for neuropathic pain relief by targeting DRG macrophage-mediated neuroinflammation. Using glycyrrhizic acid (GA) as a PI3K-targeting agent, the hydrogel was engineered to release GA in response to inflammation-associated ROS. In rats with neuropathic pain, peri-DRG administration reduced hypersensitivity, improved function, and decreased macrophage-driven neuroinflammation, demonstrating localized efficacy in pain modulation.",
  "key_findings": [
    "GPG hydrogel releases GA in response to ROS, suppressing PI3K-Akt signaling in DRG macrophages",
    "Peri-DRG administration alleviated mechanical and thermal hypersensitivity in neuropathic pain models",
    "The hydrogel reduced DRG macrophage-mediated neuroinflammation and spinal microglial activation"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo rat model of neuropathic pain",
  "scientific_significance": "This ROS-triggered natural drug delivery system provides a targeted mechanism to remodel DRG neuroinflammation, offering a minimally invasive approach for sustained neuropathic pain relief without systemic side effects."
}

{
  "summary": "This study identifies cMAF as a regulator of brain perivascular macrophages, maintaining cerebrovascular function via IGF1 production. The cMA:IGF1 pathway is upregulated in Alzheimer's disease but diminished in APOE4 carriers, suggesting a protective mechanism that may be compromised in disease progression.",
  "key_findings": [
    "cMAF regulates brain perivascular macrophages to support cerebrovascular function",
    "cMAF-IGF1 signaling is enhanced in Alzheimer's disease",
    "cMAF-IGF1 signaling is reduced in APOE4 carriers"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "The identification of cMAF as a key regulator of cerebrovascular function through perivascular macrophages provides insight into potential protective mechanisms in neurodegenerative diseases."
}

{
  "summary": "This perspective examines methanesulfonic acid (MSA) as a dual-role reagent for synthesizing porous polymer networks (PPNs), highlighting its utility as both solvent and acid catalyst to enhance scalability and reduce chemical waste. The study emphasizes MSA's potential to overcome techno-economic barriers in PPN production while maintaining robustness.",
  "key_findings": [
    "MSA functions as both solvent and acid catalyst in PPN synthesis",
    "MSA enables scalable production of robust porous polymer networks",
    "MSA reduces chemical waste and hazards in PPN fabrication"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work advances the understanding of dual-role reagents for sustainable porous polymer synthesis, providing a pathway to more industrially viable materials through simplified reaction conditions and reduced environmental impact."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates cell-enriched granular bioinks for vascular integration and endometrial repair using a cell-driven clustering strategy to create hydrogel microparticles with tunable cell density. The approach enhances stemness maintenance and angiogenic factor secretion while reducing inflammation. In vivo, constructs demonstrated improved vascular integration, reduced fibrosis, and enhanced endometrial repair compared to conventional methods.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Cell-clustered granular bioinks maintain high cell density and viability during 3D bioprinting",
    "Pre-established cellular organization improves stemness maintenance and angiogenic factor secretion",
    "Implanted constructs achieved vascular integration with host circulation and reduced fibrosis"
  ],
  "study_type": "3D bioprinting with cell-clustered gran: hydrogel microparticles",
  "scientific_significance": "This strategy provides a generalizable method for engineering transplantable tissues by preserving pre-organized cellular interactions critical for vascular integration and tissue regeneration."
}

{
  "summary": "This study proposes a 'planetary microbial systems biology' (PMSB) framework to integrate molecular, multi-omics, AI, and transdisciplinary approaches for understanding Earth's interconnected microbial systems across spatial and temporal scales. It aims to predict microbiome dynamics by federating open data and combining field observations with modeling, addressing gaps in understanding life-Earth system co-evolution.",
  "key_findings": [
    "PMSB combines molecular technologies, multi-omics data, AI, and transdisciplinary research to study microbial systems",
    "The framework enables predictive understanding of microbiome dynamics across planetary scales",
    "PMSB addresses critical knowledge gaps in life-Earth system co-evolution"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a conceptual framework for integrating diverse data and methodologies to advance predictive understanding of global microbial systems, contributing to the broader goal of elucidating Earth's biosphere dynamics through an interconnected planetary perspective."
}

{
  "summary": "This study developed polyacrylate-based dielectric elastomer fiber actuators (PA-DEFAs) using continuous coaxial wet spinning with in situ UV curing. The approach enabled scalable fabrication while maintaining high electromechanical performance, yielding actuators with up to 39% axial strain, 54.8 J kg-1 energy density, and 443 W kg- 1 peak power density. The fibers support modular integration into soft robotic systems.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Continuous fabrication of PA-DEFAs via coaxial wet spinning and in situ UV curing",
    "Maximum prestretch-free axial strain of ∼39% and energy density of 54.8 J kg-1",
    "Fibers enable modular integration into soft robotic systems like robotic arms and underwater manta-rays"
  ],
  "study_type": "Continuous fabrication strategy with coaxial wet spinning and in situ UV curing",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a scalable manufacturing platform for high-performance dielectric elastomer actuators, bridging the gap between continuous production and electromechanical performance for artificial muscles."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates optimizing NIR-II photosensitizers for phototheranostics by balancing localized and charge-transfer excited states. Using a progressive donor-acceptor intensification strategy, researchers identified a critical transition point where the excited state shifts from charge-transfer-dominated to a balanced hybrid state. This achieved NIR-II emission, oxygen-independent photodynamic activity, and 87.4% photothermal efficiency under low-power 808 nm irradiation, enabling effective tumor treatment without high laser intensity.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Critical transition from charge-transfer-dominated to balanced localized-excitation/charge-transfer hybrid excited states",
    "Oxygen-independent Type-I photodynamic activity",
    "87..4% photothermal conversion efficiency under 0.33 W/cm² 808 nm irradiation"
  ],
  "study_type": "Progressive donor-acceptor intensification strategy",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a paradigm for excited-state composition engineering in NIR-II photosensitizers, enabling phototheranostic applications under clinically relevant low-power laser conditions without reliance on hypoxic environments or high-intensity irradiation."
}

{
  "summary": "The study investigates how cells resist viral infection via innate immune sensors, identifying a supra-unitary viral genome threshold (VITREP) for replication. Using single-cell analysis and mathematical modeling, it demonstrates that viral replication requires more than one viral genome, with negative feedback regulators (NFR) suppressing immune responses to enable replication. This threshold varies by virus and cell type, independent of viral protein synthesis.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Viral replication requires a supra-unitary viral genome input (VITREP), exceeding one genome",
    "Negative feedback regulators (NFR) like DAPK1, RIOK3, and PPP2R2A suppress innate immune responses to allow replication",
    "The VITREP threshold varies across viruses and cell types, independent of viral protein synthesis"
  ],
  "study_type": "Single-cell analysis and mathematical modeling",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a fundamental mechanism where supra-unitary viral genome thresholds govern viral infectivity and tissue tropism through innate immune regulation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates thermal and stress management in sodium-ion battery hard carbon anodes by integrating negative thermal expansion oxide ZrW2O8. The approach addresses heat and strain generation during sodium ion intercalation through in situ thermal strain relief. Results show reduced heat/strain during cycling, enhanced capacity stability across wide temperature ranges (-2.0°C to 60°C), and preserved microstructure for high-rate performance.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Integration of ZrW2O8 reduces heat and strain during sodium-ion cycling",
    "Maintained capacity stability over wide temperature ranges (-20°C to 60°C)",
    "Preserved microstructure enabling high-rate cycling and long-term stability"
  ],
  "study_type": "In situ thermal strain relief strategy",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a novel internal thermal and stress management mechanism for sodium-ion battery anodes, advancing the design of safer, more stable energy storage systems through material-level thermal regulation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses challenges in liver tissue engineering by developing a photocurable hydrogel combining bovine serum albumin methacrylate with decellularized liver extracellular matrix. The hydrogel encapsulated hepatocyte spheroids to create functional engineered hepatic tissue, enhancing hepatocyte viability, polarity, and function while reducing inflammation, promoting M2 macrophage polarization, and stimulating angiogenesis. In vivo implantation in rats with liver injury demonstrated rapid vascularization, reduced fibrosis, and improved metabolic repair.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The hydrogel enhanced hepatocyte viability and polarity while maintaining hepatic functions",
    "It promoted M2 macrophage polarization and angiogenesis via VEGF/FGF upregulation and PI3K-AKT pathway activation",
    "In vivo implantation reduced liver injury, collagen deposition, and fibrosis with improved metabolic repair"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro hepatocyte spheroid encapsulation with in vivo rat implantation",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a pro-regenerative microenvironment that integrates immune modulation and vascular induction for scalable hepatic tissue engineering, advancing strategies for liver regenerative therapy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates age-dependent structural changes in Orb2 amyloid, a synaptic translation regulator in Drosophila, using cryogenic electron microscopy. It reveals a progression from translation-promoting assemblies in young flies to destabilization-resistant, translation-deficient states in aging flies, correlating with memory impairment. Manipulating chaperones shifts Orb2 states and memory outcomes, highlighting amyloid structural dynamics as a key factor in memory decline.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Orb2 amyloid transitions from translation-promoting states in young Drosophila to destabilization-resistant states in aging flies",
    "Aging shifts Orb2 amyloid ensemble toward reduced translational activity, coinciding with memory impairment",
    "Manipulating memory-related chaperones can shift Orb2 toward youthful states and improve memory in old flies"
  ],
  "study_type": "Cryogenic electron microscopy",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes an in vivo trajectory of amyloid structural evolution linked to synaptic translation and memory decline, providing a model for understanding age-related amyloid dynamics in living organisms."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated a nano-engineered approach to combat pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) recurrence after surgery by repurposing leaked pancreatic fluid (PF) as a therapeutic resource. The platform TNPs-N@B.t. + Gel combines engineered Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron to degrade tumor mucins, PF-induced enzyme corona for tumor penetration, and TRPV1 antagonist to suppress cancer-associated fibroblasts and enhance immune responses. It demonstrated reduced recurrence, prolonged survival, and pain alleviation in PDAC models without systemic toxicity.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Engineered Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron degrades tumor-associated mucins to expose PDAC cells to pancreatic enzymes",
    "Leaked pancreatic fluid forms an enzyme corona that disrupts intercellular adhesion and enhances tumor penetration",
    "TRPV1 antagonist delivery suppresses cancer-associated fibroblasts and promotes NK-cell-mediated antitumor responses"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo PDAC models",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a strategy to convert postoperative pancreatic fluid from a pathological complication into an active antitumor mechanism, providing a novel therapeutic paradigm for PDAC recurrence management."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated whether piezoelectric electroporation could enhance high-frequency irreversible electroporation (H-FIRE) for prostate cancer treatment by targeting residual tumor cells. Using a piezoelectric nanomaterial (BOIS), the researchers exploited H-FIRE-induced membrane permeability to deliver BOIS into residual cells. Upon ultrasound activation, BOIS generated an electric field and reactive oxygen species (ROS), converting reversible injury to irreversible cell death. The approach significantly improved eradication of residual tumor cells without additional external energy input.",
  "key_findings": [
    "H-FIRE-induced membrane permeability enabled intracellular delivery of piezoelectric nanomaterial BOIS into residual prostate cancer cells",
    "Ultrasound-activated BOIS generated an electric field and ROS, causing simultaneous membrane and nuclear damage",
    "This dual mechanism converted reversibly injured residual cells into irreversible lethal damage, enhancing tumor eradication"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo models",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a self-reinforcing therapeutic loop where focal ablation (H-FIRE) delivers nanomaterials to induce irreversible cell death in residual tumor cells, offering a novel strategy for complete tumor eradication post-ablation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how fatty acid desaturation influences cell fate decisions between senescence and ferroptosis. Using cellular and in vivo models, researchers found that senescent cells exhibit reduced dihomo-gamma-linolenic acid (DGLA) levels, while exogenous DGLA supplementation induces ferroptosis. The findings demonstrate DGLA's role in determining cell death pathways and show potential therapeutic implications for age-related senescence in mice and humans.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Senescent cells show reduced levels of dihomo-gamma-linolenic acid (DGLA, 20:3ω-6)",
    "Exogenous DGLA supplementation triggers ferroptosis in cells",
    "Oral DGLA reduces senescence markers in aged mice and improves functional outcomes"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo and cellular models",
  "scientific_significance": "The study identifies a specific fatty acid metabolic switch that governs the decision between senescence and ferroptosis, providing a mechanistic link between lipid metabolism and cellular aging pathways."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses inefficient N2 activation in artificial urea photosynthesis by engineering NiAu-SrTiO3 photocatalysts with bimetallic sites to tune N2 adsorption/desorption equilibria. The approach enhances N2 activation and C-N coupling, achieving 1333.63 µg gcat⁻¹ urea production and confirming *NH2 intermediate formation via in situ techniques. The findings demonstrate improved N2 utilization for ure. ",
  "key_findings": [
    "Enhanced N2 adsorption/activation via NiAu-SrTi:O3 bimetallic sites",
    "1333.63 µg gcat⁻¹ urea production rate with collectible solid-phase urea",
    "In situ confirmation of *NH2 and *NH2COO⁻ intermediates via Raman and desorption-MS"
  ],
  "study_type": "Photocatalytic urea synthesis using NiAu-SrTiO3",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a strategy for optimizing N2 activation in artificial photosynthesis, providing mechanistic insights into urea synthesis from N2, CO2, and H2O through tunable adsorption equilibria."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated the development of a targeted lipid nanoparticle (LNP) delivering transcription activator-like effector (TALE) base editors to correct KRAS mutations in non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). The approach involved designing a cancer-targeted LNP using EGFR peptide to deliver TALE base editors that convert KRAS C to T, creating a stop codon. In NSCLC mouse models, the targeted TALE LNP demonstrated effective KRAS correction, cancer targeting, and therapeutic efficacy, suggesting potential for KRAS-mutant lung cancer therapy.",
  "key_findings": [
    "TALE base editors specifically convert KRAS C to T to generate a stop codon",
    "Targeted TALE LNP using EGFR peptide achieves cancer-specific delivery",
    "Targeted TALE LNP shows therapeutic efficacy in KRAS-mutant NSCLC mouse models"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo mouse model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a targeted gene correction strategy for KRAS-mutant lung cancer using TALE base editing delivered via cancer-targeted lipid nanoparticles, providing a potential therapeutic approach for a major cancer subtype with high clinical relevance."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how industrialization impacts the human metaorgan: microbiome composition, host physiology, and their interactions across global populations. Using data from the Global Microbiome Conservancy cohort, it found industrialization correlates with homogenized microbial communities, reduced diversity, lower stability, elevated gut stress markers, increased IgA secretion, and altered IgA-bacteria interactions. Microbiome disease predictors from industrialized groups fail in less industrialized cohorts, indicating limited cross-population applicability of such models.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Industrialization correlates with homogenized gut microbial compositions",
    "Industrialization is associated with reduced microbial diversity and community stability",
    "Microbiome-based disease predictors trained on industrialized populations are uninformative in less industrialized cohorts"
  ],
  "study_type": "Multivariate cohort analysis",
  "scientific_significance": "The findings demonstrate that industrialization drives coordinated restructuring of the human metaorganism, highlighting the necessity for globally representative data to ensure equitable translational microbiome applications across diverse human populations."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the fabrication of liquid crystal elastomer (LCE) fibers with ultrafast thermal response using a tandem electrospinning alignment strategy. The approach achieved high molecular order (0.78) and exceptional actuation performance, including 80% strain, 1485% s-1 speed, and 6105 W kg-. Resonance modulation further enhanced speed by 10-fold under heating, enabling bioinspired energy efficiency.",
  "key_findings": [
    "High molecular order (0.78) in LCE fibers fabricated via tandem electrospinning alignment",
    "Actuation strain of 80% and speed of 1485% s-1 under thermal stimulation",
    "Resonance modulation increased actuation speed by 10-fold for energy efficiency optimization"
  ],
  "study_type": "Electrospinning-based alignment strategy",
  "scientific_significance": "This work advances the field of shape-memory soft materials by demonstrating a fabrication method for LCE fibers with ultrafast thermal actuation and resonance-enhanced energy efficiency, enabling applications requiring rapid, high-performance thermal responses."
}

{
  "summary": "This study developed a biomaterial thrombosis-on-a-chip model using human whole blood to evaluate acute thrombogenicity of medical devices under clinical heparin levels (0.5-5 U/mL) and shear rates (50-2000 s-1). It revealed shear-dependent fibrin events, differential fibrin responses across materials, and heparin concentration effects on fibrin aggregation without altering platelet adhesion dynamics.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Shear-dependent decrease in fibrin events on hydrophilic glass but not hydrophobic silicone",
    "Albumin pre-coating abolished fibrin on silicone but not glass",
    "Lower heparin levels (0.5 U/mL) induced more and larger fibrin aggregates compared to higher levels (5 U/mL)"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro thrombosis-on-a-chip model",
  "scientific_significance": "This model provides real-time visualization of acute biomaterial thrombosis mechanisms under clinically relevant conditions, enabling rapid assessment of anti-thrombotic material efficacy without reliance on traditional in vivo or long-term in vitro methods."
}

{
  "summary": "This study identifies a novel necrotic cell death pathway, TPNCD, driven by TECR palmitoylation. The research demonstrates that multi-site palmitoylation of TEC. The study used mouse models of acetaminophen- and concanavalin A-induced hepatotoxicity to show TPNCD contributes to acute liver injury. TPNCD was attenuated by TECR ablation or inhibition of palmitoylation/ER stress signaling.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Multi-site palmitoylation of TECR activates PERK-dependent ER stress signaling to trigger necrotic cell death",
    "TPNCD contributes to liver injury in mouse models of acetaminophen- and concanavalin A-induced hepatotoxicity",
    "Hepatocyte-specific TECR ablation or inhibition of palmitoylation/ER stress signaling attenuates TPNCD"
  ],
  "study_type": "Mouse models of hepatotoxicity",
  "scientific_significance": "The discovery of TPNCD establishes a new necrotic cell death pathway underlying acute liver injury, providing potential therapeutic targets for hepatoprotection."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates spatially decoupled charge compensation in layered metal oxide cathodes for multivalent ion batteries. By incorporating pre-intercalated melamine ligands, the cathode enables organic ligands to act as anion redox centers while metal sites handle cation redox, reducing lattice strain and improving cycle life. The material achieves over 36,000 cycles at 3 mg cm⁻² loading and supports versatile cation-anion co-intercalation.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Pre-intercalated melamine ligands enable spatially decoupled charge compensation in layered vanadium oxide cathodes",
    "The design achieves over 36,000 cycles at 3 mg cm⁻² loading with high areal capacity",
    "The cathode supports co-intercalation of multiple multivalent cations and anions"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro electrochemical characterization with layered vanadium oxide cathodes",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a novel charge-compensation mechanism that enhances structural stability and cycle life for multivalent ion batteries, advancing the feasibility of high-energy-density cathodes through spatially decoupled redox processes."
}

{
  "summary": "This study developed a biomimetic nanoparticle system (PD-DmRK) coated with tolerogenic dendritic cell membranes to enhance lymphoid organ targeting in transplantation. By combining PP242 and DHA, the system increased CCR7 expression via USF2 transcriptional activation, improving secondary lymphoid organ accumulation and prolonging allograft survival while reducing off-target sequestration.",
  "key_findings": [
    "PD-DmRK nanoparticles coated with tolerogenic dendritic cell membranes increased CCR7 expression through USF2-mediated transcriptional activation",
    "The system enhanced accumulation in secondary lymphoid organs and prolonged skin and cardiac allograft survival",
    "PD-DmRK reduced hepatic and renal sequestration and promoted regulatory T cell enrichment with reduced cytotoxic T-cell responses"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo studies",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a mechanistic link between immunoregulatory activity and CCR7-dependent lymphoid targeting, providing a biomimetic platform for targeted immunomodulation in transplantation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates genomic adaptation differences between gut bacteria in industrialized versus non-industrialized human populations. Using isolate genomes across diverse geographies and host lifestyles, it found industrialized hosts' gut bacteria exhibit expanded proteomes and increased pangenome fluidity due to recent horizontal gene transfer. Convergent adaptations in stress response, cell-envelope remodeling, and central metabolism were observed, indicating industrialization drives convergent genome evolution in gut commensals.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Industrialized human hosts' gut bacteria show expanded proteome size compared to non-industrialized populations",
    "Strains from industrialized hosts exhibit greater pangenome fluidity driven by recent horizontal gene transfer events",
    "Convergent adaptations in stress response, cell as well as central metabolism pathways were identified across industrialized populations"
  ],
  "study_type": "Genomic analysis of gut bacterial isolates across industrialized and non-industrialized human populations",
  "scientific_significance": "The study reveals industrialization drives convergent genome evolution in gut commensals, providing insights into microbial adaptation mechanisms to rapidly changing human environments."
}
